= The Baader Liberation

**A new thorn in the Federal Republic sprouts.**

Compared to its original popularity amongst the German proletariat, the Marxist cause within West Germany has largely faltered. With the banning of the KPD in 1956 by the Federal Constitutional Court; combined with the subsequent fragmentation of the non-SPD Left, the SPD has had a virtual monopoly over the working class. Its main competition on the political left, the DFU (German Peace Union), a left-wing, pacifist and neutralist political party largely funded by East Germany, has consistently failed to meet the 5% electoral threshold to qualify for seats in the Bundestag. Aside from minor armed groups scattered throughout West Germany, Marxism as a political movement could be considered to be dying out.

That is not to say that the flame of Marxism has fully been extinguished. Following a violent breakout in West Berlin, involving 6 armed plotters at the German Central Institute for Social Studies, Andreas Baader — originally arrested for arson on April 1968, freed and forced into hiding by June 1969 and November 1969 respectively, and rearrested on April 1970 — has escaped. Three of the armed plotters managed to successfully break through to the reading room which Baader had been waiting in, and after a firefight and subsequent escape through a nearby window, the group accompanying him disappeared into the alleyways of West Berlin. No one was reported dead from the incident, although an employee of the Institute named Georg Linke, and Günter Wetter; one of the court officers escorting Andreas Baader, has been hospitalized.

The incident has mostly remained a local concern, but has garnered the concern of Klaus Schütz, the Governing Mayor of Berlin. Although no official deaths were confirmed, the breakout could be the spark for a new political organization to emerge.
